WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:05.807
When you look at this, what do you see?

00:00:05.807 --> 00:00:08.349
To me,&nbsp;
this is one of the most beautiful, fascinating

00:00:08.349 --> 00:00:11.289
and frustrating things i've ever seen.

00:00:11.289 --> 00:00:18.816
That's&nbsp;
because I'm a mathematician, and these hypnotizing curly cues have haunted people like me for 150&nbsp;
years.

00:00:18.816 --> 00:00:22.379
What we're looking at is a glimpse of the Riemann hypothesis.

00:00:22.379 --> 00:00:28.217
It's one of the most important&nbsp;
-- if not THE most important -- unsolved problems in all of mathematics.

00:00:28.217 --> 00:00:32.842
You may have heard about&nbsp;
the Riemann hypothesis, because it's one of the millennium problems of the Clay Institute.

00:00:32.842 --> 00:00:37.475
What&nbsp;
that means is that the person who solves it not only achieves immortality in mathematics,

00:00:37.475 --> 00:00:41.001
but&nbsp;
also wins a 1 million prize.

00:00:41.001 --> 00:00:44.734
But there are other reasons to care about the enigma that is the&nbsp;
Riemann hypothesis.

00:00:44.734 --> 00:00:49.088
If the hypothesis is true, it would solve a mystery as old as math itself:

00:00:49.088 --> 00:00:51.360
That is, the mystery of the prime numbers.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:57.840
Countless theorems in fields as far apart as&nbsp;
cryptography and quantum physics assume that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.840 --> 00:00:59.804
the Riemann hypothesis is true,

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.965
which means that&nbsp;
we have a lot riding on this single, unproven statement.

00:01:03.965 --> 00:01:06.291
So, what is it? What is the Riemann&nbsp;
hypothesis?

00:01:06.511 --> 00:01:11.334
Why has the search for a proof become something of a holy grail for mathematicians?

00:01:11.603 --> 00:01:14.803
And what do these strange curly cues have to do with all of this?

00:01:15.805 --> 00:01:19.382
My name is Alex Kontorovich, and&nbsp;
I'm a professor of mathematics.

00:01:19.382 --> 00:01:22.613
I'll serve as your guide as we explore the Riemann hypothesis.

00:01:22.613 --> 00:01:27.004
In order to build a somewhat sophisticated understanding of this hypothesis,

00:01:27.004 --> 00:01:30.714
what I'm going&nbsp;
to try to do is mention some slightly advanced concepts.

00:01:30.714 --> 00:01:34.849
Now, you don't need an advanced degree&nbsp;
in mathematics to go on this journey with me.

00:01:34.849 --> 00:01:39.120
What I'm going to do is try to lay the groundwork,&nbsp;
step by step, in a visual and intuitive way.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:44.880
So to begin, let's venture into the chaotic&nbsp;
and elusive universe of prime numbers.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:51.760
This is factor city, where the primes are&nbsp;
literally the building blocks of all numbers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:57.136
Now, every whole number is a building, and it's&nbsp;
been constructed out of its primes.

00:01:58.383 --> 00:02:02.024
The one-story houses are exactly the primes.

00:02:02.024 --> 00:02:04.460
Now thanks to the&nbsp;
ancient Greek Euclid of Alexandria,

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:10.868
we know that the one-story buildings keep going on forever&nbsp;
-- if you can walk that far.

00:02:10.868 --> 00:02:13.512
But, depending on where you're standing, you might want to know:

00:02:13.512 --> 00:02:17.105
How&nbsp;
many one story buildings do you see around you?

00:02:17.667 --> 00:02:21.060
This is what we want to understand about the prime&nbsp;
numbers, and what we currently don't.

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:25.653
You see, the prime numbers seem to just pop up randomly among&nbsp;
the natural numbers,

00:02:25.653 --> 00:02:27.816
all the way out to infinity.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:33.760
In the late 1700s, mathematicians were&nbsp;
really starting to wonder whether it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:37.101
possible to predict the places where primes would&nbsp;
appear.

00:02:37.101 --> 00:02:39.628
Now, a teenage boy named Carl Friedrich Gauss

00:02:39.628 --> 00:02:42.067
was especially obsessed with taming the&nbsp;
primes.

00:02:42.067 --> 00:02:44.382
He calculated massive tables of primes,

00:02:44.382 --> 00:02:47.732
going all the way up to 3 million, and looked for&nbsp;
patterns.

00:02:47.732 --> 00:02:53.040
We can see the data that Gauss collected on the prime numbers by using something&nbsp;
we'll call the Prime Counting Function.

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:58.240
The graph of that function will show us exactly&nbsp;
where the prime numbers appear as the numbers get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:04.129
bigger and bigger. So, the graph stays flat until&nbsp;
you hit a prime, and at that point it jumps up by&nbsp;one.

00:03:04.398 --> 00:03:06.628
Here's what the graph looks like out to 10

00:03:06.628 --> 00:03:09.178
-- and out to 100 --

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:12.689
and now out to 3 million, as far as Gauss went.

00:03:12.982 --> 00:03:16.771
At this scale, those&nbsp;
bumps we saw early on have smoothed out,

00:03:16.771 --> 00:03:18.853
leaving a pretty pleasant curve.

00:03:19.214 --> 00:03:21.166
When Gauss&nbsp;
looked at this data, he asked himself:

00:03:21.166 --> 00:03:26.986
Is there some other function that I know that&nbsp;
has pretty much the same graph?

00:03:26.986 --> 00:03:32.708
Now, as luck would have it, at around the same time Gauss was&nbsp;
also playing around with logarithm functions.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:37.200
You might remember that a logarithm is&nbsp;
a function that undoes exponentiation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:41.141
much in the same way as division undoes&nbsp;
multiplication.

00:03:41.313 --> 00:03:45.451
What Gauss noticed was that the graph of something called the logarithmic&nbsp;
integral function

00:03:45.451 --> 00:03:48.281
(that's a function whose slope is 1 over log x)

00:03:48.281 --> 00:03:51.920
looked suspiciously similar&nbsp;
to the graph of the Prime Counting Function.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:54.080
Here's what they look like together.

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:55.860
You can see&nbsp;
why Gauss made the connection.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:59.810
At this scale, they're so close you can't even distinguish&nbsp;
them.

00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:03.613
To understand what the slope of one over log x has to do with prime numbers,

00:04:03.613 --> 00:04:06.297
let's pay&nbsp;
another visit to Factor City.

00:04:06.297 --> 00:04:10.113
What Gauss imagined was that if you're standing at address x,

00:04:10.113 --> 00:04:11.881
and&nbsp;
you look left and you look right,

00:04:11.881 --> 00:04:16.795
the proportion of one story buildings, that is primes, that&nbsp;
you would see around you

00:04:16.795 --> 00:04:21.179
is roughly the same as one over log x.

00:04:21.179 --> 00:04:24.941
This suspicion became known as&nbsp;
Gauss's Conjecture.

00:04:24.941 --> 00:04:32.272
After Gauss formulated this conjecture, his student Bernard Riemann went on to&nbsp;
do something absolutely incredible with it.

00:04:32.272 --> 00:04:37.927
But to understand what Riemann did, we first have to go&nbsp;
back in time a little bit to meet someone else.

00:04:38.938 --> 00:04:43.791
This is Leonard Euler one of the world's first&nbsp;
students of calculus.

00:04:43.791 --> 00:04:47.361
Later on in his life, he wrote many influential textbooks

00:04:47.361 --> 00:04:50.720
and standardized&nbsp;
the modern math notation that we still use to this&nbsp;day.

00:04:50.891 --> 00:04:55.823
If you've ever written something in standard&nbsp;
function notation -- you know, like y equals f x --

00:04:55.823 --> 00:04:59.292
well you can thank, or perhaps blame, Leonard&nbsp;
Euler.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:01.759
In Euler's time, mathematicians were trying

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:04.369
to understand the general structure of infinite&nbsp;
series.

00:05:04.369 --> 00:05:08.821
What that means is you're trying to add up an infinite sequence of numbers, like

00:05:08.821 --> 00:05:12.061
1, a&nbsp;
half, a quarter, an eighth, and so on, forever,

00:05:12.061 --> 00:05:16.918
sometimes that total sum is a finite number, which&nbsp;
we then call a limit.

00:05:17.872 --> 00:05:22.824
If a series has a limit we would say that the series itself is convergent.

00:05:22.824 --> 00:05:24.857
In this series, our limit is 2.

00:05:25.517 --> 00:05:31.161
On the other hand, if we were to add, let's say, one plus one plus&nbsp;
one plus one plus one forever,

00:05:31.161 --> 00:05:35.234
well that sum would eventually become larger than any finite number,

00:05:35.234 --> 00:05:38.867
and so we would say that that series is divergent.

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:44.800
Sometimes it can be rather tricky to&nbsp;
tell if a given series converges or not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:48.480
So for example, this series, which&nbsp;
is called the harmonic series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:52.880
turns out actually diverges, even though the&nbsp;
terms themselves are getting smaller and smaller.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:58.000
This is really rather not obvious, and takes&nbsp;
some mathematical gymnastics to verify.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:04.623
Now, what about this series, the sum of 1 over the&nbsp;
squares of all the positive integers?

00:06:04.623 --> 00:06:09.205
It wasn't hard for mathematicians to show that this series&nbsp;
does indeed converge.

00:06:09.205 --> 00:06:12.359
But the question was, what is the exact limit?

00:06:12.359 --> 00:06:16.808
Euler shot to mathematical&nbsp;
superstardom when he saw this problem.

00:06:16.808 --> 00:06:21.900
What he realized and proved is that the limit is&nbsp;
pi squared over six.

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:25.457
Isn't that weird? What in the world does pi have to do with the squares?

00:06:25.457 --> 00:06:27.327
Well, that's another story for another time.

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:30.253
So, full of adrenaline from his early victory,&nbsp;
Euler kept going.

00:06:30.253 --> 00:06:34.850
He found the limits of the sum of one over the positive integers to&nbsp;
the fourth power,

00:06:34.850 --> 00:06:37.443
and to the sixth power, and to the eighth power.

00:06:37.883 --> 00:06:40.015
Now, let's try to study&nbsp;
all these series at once.

00:06:40.015 --> 00:06:42.537
Let's write "s" for that exponent.

00:06:42.537 --> 00:06:45.829
When we do this, we define what's called&nbsp;
the Zeta function.

00:06:45.829 --> 00:06:50.623
In other words, Zeta of two is the sum of one over the squares,

00:06:50.623 --> 00:06:53.840
and Zeta four is&nbsp;
the sum of one over the fourth powers, and so on.

00:06:54.480 --> 00:07:00.000
Euler proved that all of these converge, but&nbsp;
only when s is greater than one.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.189
But here's where things got really interesting.

00:07:03.189 --> 00:07:09.147
Euler&nbsp;
discovered that the Zeta function could be expressed as an infinite product one -- for&nbsp;
each prime number --

00:07:09.147 --> 00:07:10.848
of an infinite series.

00:07:10.848 --> 00:07:12.044
Here's what you do:

00:07:12.044 --> 00:07:16.800
For each prime number, you add&nbsp;
up one over all the powers of that prime to the s.

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:22.240
If you multiply all those series over all&nbsp;
the prime numbers, you get the Zeta function.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:29.920
Euler's discovery hinted at an intimate&nbsp;
connection between the Zeta function and&nbsp;prime numbers.

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:33.440
But it would take another hundred&nbsp;
years for the full meaning to reveal itself.

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:39.600
And that's when our friend Bernhard Riemann would&nbsp;
make a major breakthrough in prime number theory.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:43.280
And that breakthrough would potentially&nbsp;
allow us, for the first time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:46.744
to understand the profound mysteries of&nbsp;
prime numbers.

00:07:46.744 --> 00:07:47.750
So, Riemann.

00:07:47.750 --> 00:07:51.461
He's born in 1826 in modern-day Germany.

00:07:51.461 --> 00:07:55.334
He's an absolutely&nbsp;
spectacular and innovative prolific mathematician,

00:07:55.334 --> 00:08:02.488
and, among other things, his novel approach to&nbsp;
geometry actually lays the mathematical groundwork for Einstein's theory of relativity.

00:08:02.488 --> 00:08:05.496
Riemann was&nbsp;
also one of the founders of complex analysis,

00:08:05.496 --> 00:08:09.633
which is a branch of math that studies functions&nbsp;
with complex inputs and outputs.

00:08:09.633 --> 00:08:15.280
And he realized he might have something new to say if he studied,&nbsp;
from that point of view, Euler's Zeta function.

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:21.069
You might remember that the square of any&nbsp;
real number is positive:

00:08:21.069 --> 00:08:25.789
So 2 squared is 4, but negative 2 squared is also four.

00:08:25.789 --> 00:08:28.862
So there&nbsp;
is no number whose square is negative one.

00:08:28.862 --> 00:08:34.439
It turns out that it's really rather useful for&nbsp;
mathematicians to have a number whose square is negative one.

00:08:34.439 --> 00:08:36.254
So what did we do?

00:08:36.254 --> 00:08:37.309
We just&nbsp;
made one up.

00:08:37.309 --> 00:08:40.255
We called it "I" for imaginary,

00:08:40.255 --> 00:08:44.900
so whenever you see i squared what that means is&nbsp;
you should replace it with negative 1.

00:08:44.900 --> 00:08:51.600
This means that any real multiple of i, like 3i or 2i, these&nbsp;
are all imaginary cousins of the real numbers.

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:56.400
When you take combinations of real numbers and&nbsp;
imaginary numbers, you get something we call a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:00.800
complex number. Complex numbers might look&nbsp;
like two numbers, but they're actually just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:05.000
one number expressed as two parts: The real part&nbsp;
and the imaginary.

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:09.332
The thing is, these imaginary and complex numbers they aren't imaginary at&nbsp;
all --

00:09:09.332 --> 00:09:13.906
they're just the natural extension of our usual number system from one dimension to two.

00:09:13.906 --> 00:09:18.227
They're plotted in what we call the complex plane, with the real part going left to right,

00:09:18.227 --> 00:09:19.920
and the imaginary part up to down.

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:25.244
Once you have these complex numbers, you can&nbsp;
study complex functions --

00:09:25.244 --> 00:09:30.977
that is, functions with inputs and outputs living in the complex plane.

00:09:30.977 --> 00:09:33.910
And so, armed with the power of complex analysis,

00:09:33.910 --> 00:09:38.562
Riemann opened up a whole new perspective on&nbsp;
the world of mathematics.

00:09:38.562 --> 00:09:43.749
Riemann wondered: What would happen if you allowed the Zeta&nbsp;
function to take complex inputs?

00:09:43.749 --> 00:09:47.380
That is, he allowed s to be a complex number.

00:09:47.380 --> 00:09:53.342
So let's&nbsp;
take the complex exponent s equals two plus 3i, for example.

00:09:53.342 --> 00:09:55.000
We can plug it into Zeta

00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:57.654
and the&nbsp;
function will look like this.

00:09:57.654 --> 00:10:01.142
Now, if we add each term, one at a time in the complex plane,

00:10:01.142 --> 00:10:03.840
we can&nbsp;
see how the series begins to spiral beautifully.

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:08.038
So Riemann had successfully accomplished what he&nbsp;
set out to do:

00:10:08.038 --> 00:10:11.528
He extended the Zeta function to the complex plane.

00:10:11.528 --> 00:10:18.256
But just like Euler, Riemann&nbsp;
found that the function only converged when the real part of s was greater than one.

00:10:18.256 --> 00:10:24.915
Now Riemann&nbsp;
had a brilliant idea: He realized it was possible to extend the Zeta function to the rest of the&nbsp;
complex plane.

00:10:24.915 --> 00:10:27.766
Riemann used a technique called analytic continuation,

00:10:27.766 --> 00:10:30.960
which allowed him to break&nbsp;
open the hidden potential of the Zeta function.

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:35.310
Now, analytic continuation is an advanced concept&nbsp;
in complex analysis,

00:10:35.310 --> 00:10:37.920
but it's possible to make sense of it intuitively.

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:42.668
You can think of it as&nbsp;
a logical problem-solving method to extend the domain of a function.

00:10:42.668 --> 00:10:47.120
In order for Riemann to fill&nbsp;
in the missing part of the domain in Euler's data function,

00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:49.327
he had to create a brand new function.

00:10:49.327 --> 00:10:53.792
This function would take the same values as the Zeta function, where they both converge,

00:10:53.792 --> 00:10:57.363
but it&nbsp;
had to make sense everywhere else on the plane, too.

00:10:57.363 --> 00:10:59.616
So the secret to analytic continuation is

00:10:59.616 --> 00:11:02.736
that there are really two functions at work at once.

00:11:02.736 --> 00:11:06.665
One is the original Zeta function, which has&nbsp;
limited scope.

00:11:06.665 --> 00:11:08.551
But the other is this new function,

00:11:08.551 --> 00:11:13.840
the Riemann Zeta function, which extends&nbsp;
beyond the domain that Euler defined.

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:16.746
Remember this?

00:11:16.746 --> 00:11:20.960
Now we can take a closer look at&nbsp;
it and finally begin to understand its mysteries.

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:23.863
You see, when Riemann extended the domain of the&nbsp;
Zeta function,

00:11:23.863 --> 00:11:25.495
something kind of amazing happened.

00:11:25.495 --> 00:11:31.219
In the new territory that Riemann uncovered,&nbsp;
suddenly the Zeta function can be seen crossing through the origin.

00:11:31.219 --> 00:11:35.566
What that means is for some&nbsp;
inputs, the Zeta function evaluates to zero.

00:11:35.566 --> 00:11:38.802
We call these places the "Zetas zeros".

00:11:38.802 --> 00:11:41.511
Some of&nbsp;
the Zeta zeros are easy to explain.

00:11:41.511 --> 00:11:45.558
When you input a negative even integer, the Zeta function&nbsp;
equals zero.

00:11:45.558 --> 00:11:50.560
But we don't need to worry about these so-called "trivial zeros" -- it's the&nbsp;
non-trivial zeros that we need to talk about.

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:55.509
These other Zeta zeros exhibit a very compelling&nbsp;
pattern,

00:11:55.509 --> 00:11:59.493
and that pattern is the central theme of the Riemann hypothesis.

00:11:59.493 --> 00:12:04.540
All of the non-trivial&nbsp;
zeros lie inside a single region called the critical strip.

00:12:04.540 --> 00:12:07.760
This is where the real part of s&nbsp;
is between 0 and 1.

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:12.767
Riemann proved that there are infinitely many zeros to be found in this critical&nbsp;
strip.

00:12:12.767 --> 00:12:17.732
But here is the most important takeaway from Riemann's groundbreaking 1859 paper:

00:12:17.732 --> 00:12:23.220
Riemann&nbsp;
hypothesized that all of the non-trivial zeros will lie not just somewhere in the strip,

00:12:23.220 --> 00:12:25.142
but&nbsp;
on a single vertical line,

00:12:25.142 --> 00:12:26.730
smack dab in the middle.

00:12:26.730 --> 00:12:31.520
We call this the "critical line" which&nbsp;
is where the real part of s is exactly one half.

00:12:32.480 --> 00:12:37.840
This is exactly the hypothesis that now bears&nbsp;
Riemann's name and the million dollar bounty.

00:12:40.240 --> 00:12:44.240
Now at this point, you might be wondering:&nbsp;
Why does the location of these non-trivial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:48.365
zeros matter, and what does all of this have&nbsp;
to do with prime numbers?

00:12:48.365 --> 00:12:49.601
I don't blame you.

00:12:49.601 --> 00:12:54.789
Let me show you exactly why Riemann's hypothesis&nbsp;
is of such profound consequence to number theory.

00:12:54.789 --> 00:12:57.600
And let me do so by sharing&nbsp;
Riemann's final discovery.

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:03.172
Remember Gauss's prime counting function, from the&nbsp;
very beginning of the video?

00:13:03.172 --> 00:13:06.892
Well, for technical reasons we're going to slightly modify it right&nbsp;
now,

00:13:06.892 --> 00:13:10.498
but don't worry. We'll preserve the essence of the function.

00:13:10.498 --> 00:13:14.090
instead of stepping up by 1 every&nbsp;
time we see a new prime number,

00:13:14.090 --> 00:13:17.698
which we'll call p, let us step up by log p.

00:13:17.698 --> 00:13:23.354
And we'll also do the&nbsp;
same when we encounter p squared, and p cubed, and p to the fourth, and so on.

00:13:23.354 --> 00:13:26.046
So let me&nbsp;
try to explain why we're doing all of this.

00:13:26.046 --> 00:13:32.976
Riemann found a surprising connection between&nbsp;
this modification of Gauss's conjecture and his new Zeta function.

00:13:32.976 --> 00:13:36.578
He discovered a wave that&nbsp;
corresponds to the Zeta function at s equals one,

00:13:36.578 --> 00:13:42.680
and showed that Gauss's conjecture would follow,&nbsp;
if only you could show that this wave was the fundamental frequency,

00:13:42.680 --> 00:13:48.751
which means that it more&nbsp;
and more closely approximates the prime counting function as they both grow to infinity.

00:13:48.751 --> 00:13:53.681
So here's&nbsp;
what the prime counting function looks like with that wave that Riemann discovered.

00:13:53.681 --> 00:13:59.274
Riemann&nbsp;
realized that the Zeta zeros were exactly what he needed to adjust this straight line

00:13:59.274 --> 00:14:01.572
to match the&nbsp;
jagged counting function.

00:14:01.572 --> 00:14:05.902
Every time he added a Zeta zero, it contributed a harmonic.

00:14:05.902 --> 00:14:08.240
Here's what&nbsp;
the first harmonic looks like when we add it on.

00:14:08.960 --> 00:14:13.840
It ever so gently tries to smooth out the&nbsp;
wiggles of the modified prime counting function.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:18.640
what happens when we plug in the&nbsp;
second Zeta zero? Little better, right?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.640 --> 00:14:24.480
What about the first 10, or the first&nbsp;
30, or the first 60? You get the point.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:30.080
Riemann was able to rigorously prove that if&nbsp;
you add up all the harmonics of the Zeta zeros,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.080 --> 00:14:31.718
all infinitely many of them,

00:14:31.718 --> 00:14:35.280
you get a perfect&nbsp;
match to Gauss's modified prime counting function.

00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:41.920
So, Riemann's hypothesis showed that the&nbsp;
distribution of prime numbers can in fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:43.305
be predicted.

00:14:43.305 --> 00:14:49.765
The location of prime numbers is&nbsp;
profoundly connected to the location of these non-trivial Zeta zeros.

00:14:49.765 --> 00:14:53.106
This means that if the&nbsp;
Riemann hypothesis is indeed true,

00:14:53.106 --> 00:14:57.840
it would tell us everything we could possibly have a right to&nbsp;
know about the distribution of prime numbers.

00:14:58.960 --> 00:15:02.877
Needless to say, Riemann was unable to&nbsp;
prove the full hypothesis.

00:15:02.877 --> 00:15:08.857
In other words, neither he nor anyone since has been able to show&nbsp;
that every single non-trivial Zeta zero

00:15:08.857 --> 00:15:11.927
lies on that critical line.

00:15:11.927 --> 00:15:17.639
At one point, a massive&nbsp;
computing project checked over one billion non-trivial Zeta zeros

00:15:17.639 --> 00:15:18.986
every single day.

00:15:18.986 --> 00:15:21.908
The computer was looking for a single errant zero.

00:15:21.908 --> 00:15:27.335
If it found just one, then the Riemann&nbsp;
hypothesis would fall apart.

00:15:27.335 --> 00:15:31.460
In the end, of the 10 trillion zeros that the computer checked,

00:15:31.460 --> 00:15:33.840
every single one was on the critical line.

00:15:36.400 --> 00:15:39.213
But if you see a pattern holding again and&nbsp;
again and again,

00:15:39.213 --> 00:15:43.383
it's still impossible for machines to check it all the way to infinity.

00:15:43.383 --> 00:15:47.655
So&nbsp;
brute force computation will never resolve the Riemann hypothesis.

00:15:47.655 --> 00:15:50.336
That means there's&nbsp;
still only one way to be sure of it,

00:15:50.336 --> 00:15:53.764
and it's the same way that the ancient Greeks did&nbsp;
their math:

00:15:53.764 --> 00:16:07.840
rigorous absolute mathematical proof.

